seg-39ab7752d86d = Quantum-sensor models: what Kshana models, and what it does not

# text
seg-daf0e0c0b16f = Kshana is a <b>PNT-resilience (PNT: positioning, navigation and timing) simulator with quantum-sensor performance models</b>. It is <em>not</em> a first-principles quantum-physics simulator. This page states exactly what that means so the "quantum" framing cannot be mistaken for more than it is.

# text
seg-6e504d97c41e = What IS modelled

# text
seg-7884c9a7d883 = Each quantum (and classical) sensor is an <b>error model</b> driven by published noise-budget parameters:

# text
# c1: ClockClass
# c2: VALIDATION.md
# c3: VERIFICATION-MATRIX.md
seg-cad22f39d579 = <b>Clocks</b> — white-frequency and random-walk-frequency PSDs (power spectral densities), a flicker (1/f) Allan floor, and linear drift, sourced from datasheets and papers: the CSAC (chip-scale atomic clock) Microchip SA.45s; the strontium optical-lattice clock of Origlia et al. (arXiv:{ "1503.08457" }), a space-oriented goal; and the six <c1/> defaults (CSAC; USO, ultra-stable oscillator; DSAC, Deep Space Atomic Clock; TCXO, temperature-compensated crystal oscillator; OCXO, oven-controlled crystal oscillator; RAFS, rubidium atomic frequency standard), each citing one source. The Allan deviation these produce is validated against the standard noise-type slopes (see <a1><c2/></a1>). One optical-clock curve is real: the measured ⁸⁸Sr tweezer-clock Allan deviation of Norcia et al. (<em>Science</em> { "2019" }) is reproduced by the noise fit, a VALIDATED row in <a2><c3/></a2>; the holdover figures built on the optical class stay MODELLED.

# text
# c1: inertial
# c2: gnss-ins
# c3: hybrid-ukf
# c4: src/inertial/
seg-13c3340e773b = <b>Inertial sensors</b> — in the <c1/> and hybrid packs, a single-axis (1-DOF, one degree of freedom) accelerometer/gyro error budget: velocity random walk, angular random walk, acceleration random walk, and an Allan bias-instability floor. The <c2/> and <c3/> kinds instead run the three-axis strapdown mechanisation in <c4/> (quaternion attitude, north-east-down navigation equations, a deterministic IMU error model).

# text
# c1: quantum-trade
# c2: quantum-time-transfer
# c3: quantum-anomaly-detect
# c4: quantum-gnss-free-nav
# c5: hybrid-ukf
# c6: quantum-time-transfer
# c7: quantum-anomaly-detect
# c8: quantum-gnss-free-nav
seg-f361d586b348 = The quantum scenario kinds — <c1/>, <c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/> and <c5/> — are built from these error models and are MODELLED; <c6/>, <c7/> and <c8/> also report a representativeness record with the gaps to flight.

# text
seg-05bbaddd4df6 = The engine is <b>neutral</b>: "quantum" and "classical" are the same code path with different coefficients. The contrast in the figures of merit comes entirely from the input noise parameters, each traceable to a cited source.

# text
seg-a1a067588520 = What is NOT modelled

# text
seg-49a656243210 = For the clock and time-transfer sensors, none of the underlying quantum physics is simulated — only the net Allan contribution, supplied as a coefficient. For the <b>cold-atom accelerometer</b> a first-principles layer now exists (see below); the remaining systematics are still coefficient-level or unmodelled:

# text
# c1: Φ = k_eff·a·T²
# c2: σ_Φ = 1/(C·√N)
# c3: |H(ω)| = (4/ω²)sin²(ωT/2)
# c4: σ_Φ² = k_eff²·S_a·T³/3
# c5: src/inertial/quantum_imu.rs
# c6: q_va
# c7: a_max = π/(k_eff·T²)
# c8: QUANTUM.md
seg-844e65d52572 = the atom-interferometer <b>Mach–Zehnder phase</b> (<c1/>), <b>quantum projection / shot noise</b> (<c2/>), interferometer <b>contrast</b> decay, <b>cycle time</b>, and the <b>vibration-coupling transfer function</b> (<c3/>, white-PSD variance <c4/>) <em>are now modelled from first principles</em> for the CAI (cold-atom interferometer) accelerometer (<c5/>), deriving the white-acceleration PSD <c6/> the classical model consumes, together with the <b>fringe-ambiguity dynamic range</b> (<c7/>) — see <a1><c8/></a1>;

# text
# c1: coriolis_phase
# c2: coriolis_accel_bias
# c3: 2·v⊥·Ω
# c4: ac_stark_phase
# c5: src/inertial/quantum_imu.rs
seg-71a782041b89 = the <b>Coriolis</b> systematic (<c1/> / <c2/>, the <c3/> cross-coupling) and the <b>AC-Stark (AC: alternating-current) / light-shift</b> systematic (<c4/>, which cancels under a symmetric two-photon detuning) <em>are now modelled and unit-tested</em> in <c5/>;

# text
