seg-00c510117abc = three further CAI error terms <em>are now modelled</em> as library functions in <c1/> ({ "0.36" }; MODELLED; not yet reachable from a scenario kind):

# text
# c1: σ_Φ² = (π/2)²·S_φ⁰/τ_R
# c2: t_d = 2L/c
seg-fcaf6ff00974 = <b>Raman laser-phase noise</b>: the sensitivity-function weighting of Cheinet et al. ({ "2008" }), Eq. { "8" }, with the white-phase closed form <c1/> (Eq. { "18" } at one cycle) and a numeric route for any phase-noise spectrum, plus the laser frequency noise that the retro-reflection delay <c2/> does not cancel (Le Gouët et al. { "2007" }, Eqs. { "9" } to { "11" });

# text
# c1: δg = σ_v²/R
# c2: R
seg-6e2236c81db8 = <b>wavefront curvature</b> (pure defocus only): the bias <c1/> of a thermal cloud on a beam of curvature radius <c2/>, and its reduction when only atoms inside a detection radius are counted (after Louchet-Chauvet et al. { "2011" } and Schkolnik et al. { "2015" }); higher-order aberrations are not modelled;

# text
# c1: erfc(π/(√2·s))
seg-92295ad17228 = <b>fringe-ambiguity resolution</b>: the probability of a wrong fringe index, <c1/>, for a classical-accelerometer aid (Lautier et al. { "2014" }) and for two interrogation times (after Bonnin et al. { "2018" }), with the inverse that gives the largest classical noise for a stated failure probability.

# text
# c1: τ_R
seg-f383678fb806 = Each closed form is checked against an independent numerical route (frequency-domain quadrature, or a Monte Carlo over atoms or shots) and its limiting cases. The one published number reproduced is Cheinet et al.'s white-phase-noise level: −{ "111" } dB·rad²/Hz at <c1/> = { "20" } µs gives about { "1" } mrad per shot ({ "0.99" } mrad; the paper derives that level with its Eq. { "17" }, flicker plus white). None of this is a measurement of any instrument;

# text
seg-150f8b9dfea4 = still <b>not</b> modelled: higher-order wavefront aberrations, the phase noise of a specific laser system, and clock-side first-principles physics;

